// A `@doc { … }` text tier (object-model slice 6f) holds verbatim prose, not code: its body is
// captured un-parsed (so markdown punctuation never lexes as tokens) and, on a normal run, the
// block is stripped before lowering exactly like an inactive code tier — it produces no output and
// reaches neither backend's IR, so the differential agrees by construction. Only `lang doc` reads
// it. Here the prose deliberately contains characters that are not valid tokens on their own
// (`#`, `*`, a stray `%`, an unmatched-looking quote inside backticks); the program still runs.
// expect: stdout "3"
// expect: stdout "done"
// expect: exit 0
@doc {
    # Adder

    `add(a, b)` returns **a + b**. 100% pure; no side effects.
}

fn add(a: int, b: int): int { return a + b }

echo add(1, 2)
echo "done"
